  "body": {
    "id": "lem:oracle-leading-permutation-symmetry",
    "kind": "lemma",
    "statement": "For every law \\(P\\), sample size \\(N\\), and finite quantile set \\(\\mathcal T\\), the oracle statistic \\(\\mathsf S_{P,N}\\) in \\(\\text{def:oracle-leading-statistic}\\) is a measurable function of \\((O_1,\\ldots,O_N)\\) invariant under every permutation of the \\(N\\) observations.",
    "free_symbols": [
      "P",
      "N",
      "\\mathcal T",
      "\\mathsf S_{P,N}",
      "O_i"
    ],
    "depends_on": [
      "def:oracle-leading-statistic"
    ],
    "proof_tex": "For a heavy arm, every coordinate of \\(\\mathsf S_{a,P,N}\\) is a deterministic population constant times a sum of the same measurable function of each \\(O_i\\), minus the corresponding deterministic expectation.  For a Gaussian arm it is a deterministic multiple of \\(\\sum_{i=1}^N\\mathrm{IF}_{a,P}(O_i;\\tau)\\).  Finite sums of measurable functions are measurable, and reindexing either sum by a permutation leaves it unchanged.  Concatenating the two arm vectors preserves both properties.  Hence \\(\\mathsf S_{P,N}\\) is measurable and permutation symmetric.",
